      "end": "finished",
      "end_summary": "Fixed the issue with invalid `self.instance` when validating serializers using `many=True`. The problem was that during bulk validation, child serializers were receiving the complete list of instances as `self.instance` instead of the individual instance for each item being validated.\n\nMade two key changes:\n1. Modified `ListSerializer.to_internal_value()` in `rest_framework/serializers.py` to:\n   - Validate that instance and data lists have matching lengths (raising AssertionError if mismatched)\n   - For each data item, temporarily bind the child serializer's `instance` to the corresponding item from the instance list\n   - Properly restore the original instance after validation\n\n2. Updated the test `tests/test_serializer.py::TestMultipleObjectsValidation::test_exception_raised_when_data_and_instance_length_different` to correctly expect the AssertionError during `is_valid()` call (where validation actually occurs) rather than during serializer construction.\n\nThe fix ensures that serializers with custom validation logic that depends on `self.instance` (like checking `self.instance.is_valid` in the example) work correctly with both single instances and bulk operations using `many=True`.",
